T031: last-resort path with ota_fallback_slot, first hardware boot, 2026-10-30
Source: serial console capture pasted by the operator (timestamps are the host's).

Image: the build whose ota_last_resort compared the variable with `itest.s ... eq B`.
BOOT_ORDER=A, slot A out of attempts.

[2026-10-30 22:51:57] OTA: no eligible slot, BOOT_ORDER=A
[2026-10-30 22:51:57] Unknown operator 'eq'
[2026-10-30 22:51:57] OTA: last resort, booting slot A without spending an attempt
[2026-10-30 22:51:57] 7668208 bytes read in 489 ms (15 MiB/s)

What it shows
1. The path still boots slot A with the default, because an `itest` that fails leaves the slot at A.
   That is the fail-safe working, and the reason a mistyped value cannot strand the device.
2. The operator was wrong: U-Boot's itest accepts -lt -gt -eq -ne -ge -le and < > == != <>
   (cmd/itest.c op_table), not `eq`. As built, ota_fallback_slot=B could never have taken effect.
3. A host `make check` and a green build did not see this; only the board did.

Fix: `-eq`, the same spelling the script already uses for `-gt`. Rebuilt; the seeded uboot-env.bin
and the SPL both carry it. NOT yet run on a board: neither the default path with the fix nor
ota_fallback_slot=B has been booted since.
